{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1280,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you’re in scorching heat, or when your \nbody is working hard and you’ve got hot,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4960,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hot sweat all over, sticky and stifling \n- does wiping off the sweat help you cool  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9520,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "off? Or is it better to leave it on?\nObviously we’re ignoring all social  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13040,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "norms \u0026 medical advice and just talking \nfrom a pure physics perspective... and in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16320,
    "dDurationMs": 3320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physics there are two main ways you can \nuse water alone to cool something down. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19640,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The first water cooling method is to run cool \nwater over or through the hot object so the water  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23680,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "warms up and takes away the heat (and therefore \ncools the object down) - this direct transfer of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28040,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "heat is the basic idea behind the radiators in car \nengines, liquid cooling of fancy gaming computers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32760,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and water cooling of power plants. For a \nhuman being, this would mean drinking, say,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36600,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a liter of freezing-cold water every hour, letting \nyour body heat the water up to body temperature,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41000,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and then removing it from your body by urinating \nor sweating and then wiping off the sweat. A liter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45240,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of water can move 1 Calorie of heat per degree \nCelsius it heats up, so using water for liquid  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49280,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cooling can remove at most around 37 Calories of \nheat every hour - one Calorie for every degree  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53800,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Celsius between freezing and body temperature.\nThe second water cooling method is to put water  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58120,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the object and let it evaporate. A liter of \nwater, to evaporate, absorbs around 540 Calories  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63000,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of heat. Which is a lot more than the 37 Calories \nremoved by drinking \u0026 urinating ice water. But a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68000,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "human body can’t necessarily evaporate a liter \nof water every hour: a square meter, under room  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72280,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "temperature conditions, will only evaporate about \na third of a liter per hour, and the surface area  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76000,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a human is only between 1 and 2 square meters. \nSo we could expect a sweaty human body to lose at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80760,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most maybe 180-360 Calories of heat every hour \nfrom evaporative cooling. Which is still 5 to 10  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86160,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "times more cooling power than liquid cooling.\nObviously evaporation is also super  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89840,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "condition-dependent: if it’s hotter or windier \nor drier, then the power of evaporative cooling  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93800,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "increases substantially, while if it’s cooler or \nmore humid then evaporation is less effective. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98480,
    "dDurationMs": 3400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But either way, it seems pretty safe to say \nthat for human bodies, evaporative cooling  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101880,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is far more effective than liquid cooling. In \nother words, letting your sweat evaporate is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105520,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "far more effective than wiping it off.\nWhich actually isn’t that surprising:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108760,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sweating is our evolutionary means of cooling off, \nso perhaps it’s not surprising that letting your  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112680,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "body do its thing turns out to be a good idea... \nbut it’s amazing how effective it really is. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116840,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And luckily your body can use both methods \nof water cooling: if you drink cold water,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120840,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you benefit from direct heat transfer while your \nbody warms the water up to body temperature,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124600,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and then you benefit from evaporative \ncooling when the water evaporates as sweat. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127840,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And physics doesn’t completely prohibit wiping \noff your sweat. If sweat is literally dripping  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131400,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "off of you a) it’s not going to help evaporatively \ncool you once it’s on the ground, and b) chances  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135840,
    "dDurationMs": 3320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are you’re producing sweat faster than it can \nevaporate. So it’s probably ok to wipe some of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139160,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the sweat off - or perhaps better yet, smear \nit around to make sure it has the most surface  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143440,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "area possible for the most effective cooling!\nAs a reminder - nothing in this video constitutes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147200,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "medical or hygiene advice.\nBut if your sweat is dripping,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150640,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s much better – for the purposes of evaporative \ncooling – to smear it around rather than wipe it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154760,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "off entirely. And if you’re hot and sweaty but \nit’s not dripping, physics says “leave it on.” "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161360,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alright, we’ve now covered “is it better to walk \nor run in the rain?“, “should you walk or run when  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164880,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s cold?” “should you wipe off your sweat?” \nand “should you donate to the Against Malaria  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168360,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Foundation or Hellen Keller International?” \nActually no that last one is the sponsor of this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172080,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "video: GiveWell, the non-profit that researches \n\u0026 vets other charities for you. In particular,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176440,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "GiveWell searches out the non-profits that \nimmediately and directly benefit or save  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179520,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the most peoples’ lives for the least money, \nlike health \u0026 economic programs in developing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183360,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "countries. You can look at GiveWell’s full charity \nrecommendation list on their website (the list  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187000,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "currently includes both the Against Malaria \nFoundation AND Hellen Keller International),  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191280,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and you can also give directly to any of those \ncharities (or to a fund directed to the current  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194560,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "top choices) through GiveWell; 100% of your \ndonation goes to the charities. On top of that,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 198520,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if you’re a first time donor, Givewell will \nmatch your donation dollar for dollar up to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202040,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "100 dollars . Go to Givewell.org/minutephysics \nto donate, and to make sure your donation gets  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206360,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matched, at checkout on the “how did you hear \nabout GiveWell” question, select “YouTube”  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210000,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and then “MinutePhysics”. Again, that’s \n“Youtube” with fundraiser code minutephysics  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213920,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and your donation to GiveWell’s recommended \ncharities will get matched up to 100 dollars."
    } ]
  } ]
}
